WEBVTT

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.557
Open the nyc taxi project.

00:00:05.837 --> 00:00:10.970
First, load the zone lookup into DuckDB: open the Project menu, and choose Seed.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:19.234
dbt reads the CSV, and inserts two hundred sixty-five rows into a table called taxi zone lookup.

00:00:20.184 --> 00:00:24.864
To use one model inside another, write ref, and the name of the model or seed.

00:00:25.144 --> 00:00:30.317
Not the table name: dbt fills that in, for whichever database you run against.

00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:39.029
Each ref is also a dependency. dbt draws the graph from them, and always builds trips before the models that use it.

00:00:39.979 --> 00:00:43.329
Right-click models, New File: trips per month dot sql.

00:00:46.911 --> 00:00:50.620
Count the trips and the average fare for each month and pickup borough.

00:00:55.511 --> 00:01:00.685
From ref trips, joined to ref taxi zone lookup, to get the borough of each pickup zone.

00:01:00.965 --> 00:01:02.227
Group by all, and save.

00:01:03.177 --> 00:01:06.119
A second model: top pickup zones dot sql.

00:01:12.263 --> 00:01:15.868
This time, start with a Jinja variable: set top n to ten.

00:01:16.818 --> 00:01:22.346
Then count the trips for each pickup zone, with the zone's name and borough, from the same two refs.

00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:28.627
Order by trips, busiest first, and limit to top n.

00:01:28.907 --> 00:01:29.506
Save.

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:32.337
Jinja has three kinds of braces.

00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:34.595
Double curly braces print a value.

00:01:34.875 --> 00:01:37.571
Curly brace and percent run logic, like set.

00:01:37.851 --> 00:01:39.818
And curly brace and hash is a comment.

00:01:40.768 --> 00:01:44.712
When dbt compiles a model, all of it turns into plain SQL.

00:01:44.992 --> 00:01:45.822
Let's see that.

00:01:46.772 --> 00:01:50.819
With top pickup zones open, open the Model menu, and choose Compile.

00:01:53.011 --> 00:01:55.656
Studio opens the compiled SQL in a window.

00:01:55.936 --> 00:01:58.313
The first tab shows what we wrote, with the Jinja.

00:01:59.263 --> 00:02:00.687
Switch to Compiled SQL.

00:02:00.967 --> 00:02:06.438
Both refs became full table names in the dev database, and limit top n became limit ten.

00:02:07.388 --> 00:02:09.525
This is the SQL DuckDB will run.

00:02:09.805 --> 00:02:10.757
Close the window.

00:02:11.707 --> 00:02:14.443
Now run the whole project: Project menu, Run.

00:02:15.393 --> 00:02:19.795
dbt builds trips first, and only then the two models that depend on it.

00:02:20.075 --> 00:02:21.561
Three models, no errors.

00:02:22.511 --> 00:02:23.745
Now look at the result.

00:02:24.025 --> 00:02:26.896
With top pickup zones open, choose Model, Preview.

00:02:29.108 --> 00:02:30.852
Ten rows, because of top n.

00:02:31.132 --> 00:02:37.908
East Harlem North is the busiest pickup zone of 2025, with a hundred forty-three thousand trips.

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:43.787
Close it, and open Run History: the seed and the run are listed, with their status.

00:02:44.737 --> 00:02:45.827
Open the Git tab.

00:02:46.107 --> 00:02:47.590
The two new models are listed.

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:54.356
Stage all, type a message, add trips per month and top pickup zones, and commit.

00:02:57.859 --> 00:02:59.761
Sync Changes pushes it to GitHub.

00:03:02.959 --> 00:03:05.344
To recap: a seed loads a CSV.

00:03:05.624 --> 00:03:11.270
Ref names a model and records the dependency, so dbt runs things in the right order.

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:15.719
Jinja adds variables and logic, and compile shows the plain SQL.

00:03:15.999 --> 00:03:18.450
Next, we describe our models with properties.
